WEBVTT

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:03.009
Last time, the model of the atom changed

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:03.952
three times,

00:00:04.514 --> 00:00:06.521
each time because of evidence that didn't fit.

00:00:07.263 --> 00:00:09.368
Your own investigations work the same way.

00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:12.360
Remember the sand and salt mixture, where the

00:00:12.401 --> 00:00:14.127
recovered masses added up to more than a

00:00:14.148 --> 00:00:14.669
hundred percent?

00:00:15.373 --> 00:00:17.810
That extra wasn't a maths mistake.

00:00:18.415 --> 00:00:20.520
It was new evidence, and it changed the

00:00:20.561 --> 00:00:20.921
question,

00:00:21.463 --> 00:00:23.969
from how much salt is there, to was

00:00:24.031 --> 00:00:25.434
the sand really dry?

00:00:26.136 --> 00:00:28.459
Today, we'll build a chemistry question and a

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.584
hypothesis, then let new evidence improve them.

00:00:32.381 --> 00:00:35.126
In the percentage video, heating drove the water

00:00:35.246 --> 00:00:37.550
out of blue copper sulfate crystals, leaving a

00:00:37.811 --> 00:00:38.532
white powder.

00:00:39.193 --> 00:00:40.335
That's an observation,

00:00:40.878 --> 00:00:43.162
something we notice with our senses or instruments.

00:00:43.885 --> 00:00:45.772
To investigate it, we turn it into an

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.765
inquiry question, one that data can answer.

00:00:49.531 --> 00:00:51.017
What happens when you heat crystals?

00:00:51.741 --> 00:00:54.188
That's too broad, because there's nothing specific to

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:54.489
measure.

00:00:55.112 --> 00:00:57.295
A better question is: what percentage of the

00:00:57.336 --> 00:00:59.559
mass of blue copper sulfate crystals is lost

00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:02.184
when they're heated until their mass stops changing?

00:01:03.007 --> 00:01:04.990
It names what we measure, the mass before

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:06.473
and after, and the condition,

00:01:06.995 --> 00:01:08.177
heating to a steady mass.

00:01:09.022 --> 00:01:10.749
Next comes a hypothesis,

00:01:11.311 --> 00:01:13.480
a testable prediction with a reason from what

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.243
we already know.

00:01:15.085 --> 00:01:16.530
A useful pattern is if,

00:01:17.031 --> 00:01:18.094
then, because.

00:01:18.936 --> 00:01:21.119
If blue copper sulfate crystals are heated until

00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:24.063
their mass stops changing, then they'll lose thirty-six

00:01:24.103 --> 00:01:26.025
point one percent of their mass, because the

00:01:26.065 --> 00:01:28.629
formula shows that water is thirty-six point one

00:01:28.669 --> 00:01:31.132
percent of the mass, and heating drives the

00:01:31.172 --> 00:01:31.653
water off.

00:01:32.456 --> 00:01:34.361
The reason is the part that makes it

00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:35.164
more than a guess.

00:01:35.907 --> 00:01:37.150
If, then, because:

00:01:37.752 --> 00:01:39.336
the because is the chemistry.

00:01:41.981 --> 00:01:45.068
Primary data are measurements you collect yourself, like

00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:46.632
masses read from a balance.

00:01:47.293 --> 00:01:49.994
Secondary data were collected by someone else, like

00:01:50.035 --> 00:01:52.835
the relative atomic masses behind our thirty-six point

00:01:52.956 --> 00:01:55.177
one percent, from a published data sheet.

00:01:55.758 --> 00:01:57.684
And some questions should only be answered with

00:01:57.785 --> 00:01:58.667
secondary data.

00:01:59.492 --> 00:02:02.146
At what temperature does copper sulfate itself start

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:02.709
to break down?

00:02:03.473 --> 00:02:05.914
Testing that would mean very strong heating, which

00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:08.375
gives off harmful gases, so we'd look it

00:02:08.416 --> 00:02:10.577
up in published data from a reliable source.

00:02:11.624 --> 00:02:13.516
So how do we judge a hypothesis?

00:02:14.362 --> 00:02:15.166
Is it testable,

00:02:15.669 --> 00:02:17.417
with something we can change or measure?

00:02:18.141 --> 00:02:20.211
Is it specific enough to answer the inquiry

00:02:20.231 --> 00:02:22.079
question, which asks for a percentage?

00:02:22.884 --> 00:02:24.579
Is the reason sound chemistry?

00:02:25.224 --> 00:02:27.138
And could some result show it's wrong?

00:02:28.226 --> 00:02:28.930
Here's a weaker one.

00:02:29.495 --> 00:02:31.590
The crystals will lose some mass when they're

00:02:31.631 --> 00:02:31.892
heated.

00:02:32.578 --> 00:02:33.583
Which check does it fail?

00:02:37.195 --> 00:02:39.104
It's testable, and it could even be shown

00:02:39.206 --> 00:02:41.015
wrong, if the mass didn't change.

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:43.465
But it gives no amount, so it isn't

00:02:43.526 --> 00:02:45.070
specific enough for our question.

00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:47.121
It also gives no reason.

00:02:48.006 --> 00:02:50.869
Our hypothesis passes all four checks, because a

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.392
result of twenty-five percent would clearly show it's

00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:53.673
wrong.

00:02:54.321 --> 00:02:55.449
Now the new evidence.

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:58.784
Three groups each heated two point five zero

00:02:58.845 --> 00:02:59.729
grams of crystals.

00:03:00.311 --> 00:03:02.756
Group A heated gently until the mass stopped

00:03:02.836 --> 00:03:05.462
changing, and lost thirty-six point zero percent.

00:03:05.928 --> 00:03:06.355
That fits.

00:03:07.143 --> 00:03:09.612
Group B stopped after four minutes, with blue

00:03:09.672 --> 00:03:12.542
patches still showing, and lost only twenty-seven point

00:03:12.582 --> 00:03:13.104
six percent.

00:03:13.849 --> 00:03:16.022
Group C used a roaring flame.

00:03:16.405 --> 00:03:18.413
Their powder turned black at the bottom, and

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:20.502
they lost forty-seven point two percent.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.596
Which one should change our hypothesis, and which

00:03:23.678 --> 00:03:25.043
one just points to a problem with the

00:03:25.084 --> 00:03:25.324
method?

00:03:29.156 --> 00:03:30.642
Group B is a method problem.

00:03:31.125 --> 00:03:33.007
The blue patches show water was still there,

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:35.352
so they didn't heat until the mass stopped

00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:37.936
changing, which our hypothesis already requires.

00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:41.225
It doesn't challenge the hypothesis, but it tells

00:03:41.266 --> 00:03:42.328
us that time matters.

00:03:43.112 --> 00:03:44.158
Group C is different.

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:46.545
They did heat until the mass was steady,

00:03:46.907 --> 00:03:48.691
and they still lost far more than the

00:03:48.732 --> 00:03:49.032
water.

00:03:49.694 --> 00:03:52.602
Black solid means the copper sulfate itself broke

00:03:52.643 --> 00:03:52.862
down.

00:03:53.645 --> 00:03:55.489
So the because was incomplete,

00:03:56.051 --> 00:03:58.316
heating doesn't always drive off only water.

00:03:59.079 --> 00:04:00.160
When evidence doesn't fit,

00:04:00.682 --> 00:04:03.407
ask what it challenges: the method, the prediction,

00:04:03.789 --> 00:04:04.309
or the reason.

00:04:05.145 --> 00:04:06.236
Published data agree.

00:04:06.802 --> 00:04:09.031
Copper sulfate breaks down only when it's much

00:04:09.152 --> 00:04:10.920
hotter than it needs to be to lose

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:11.382
its water.

00:04:12.166 --> 00:04:14.619
So we modify the hypothesis, with a condition

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:15.564
and a better reason.

00:04:16.405 --> 00:04:18.466
If the crystals are heated gently until their

00:04:18.486 --> 00:04:21.488
mass stops changing, then they'll lose thirty-six point

00:04:21.568 --> 00:04:23.889
one percent of their mass, because gentle heat

00:04:23.989 --> 00:04:26.470
drives off the water, while strong heat also

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:27.711
breaks down the copper sulfate.

00:04:28.595 --> 00:04:30.628
And Group B's evidence gives us a sharper

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:31.010
question.

00:04:31.554 --> 00:04:33.879
How does the time of gentle heating affect

00:04:33.941 --> 00:04:35.303
the percentage of mass lost?

00:04:36.026 --> 00:04:38.791
Our prediction: the percentage rises, then levels off

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:40.976
at thirty-six point one, once all the water

00:04:40.996 --> 00:04:41.316
has gone.

00:04:41.869 --> 00:04:42.210
Your turn.

00:04:43.097 --> 00:04:45.618
A student dissolves a sand and salt mixture

00:04:45.799 --> 00:04:47.400
in twenty millilitres of water,

00:04:48.001 --> 00:04:50.502
filters it, and recovers much less salt than

00:04:50.544 --> 00:04:50.943
expected.

00:04:51.786 --> 00:04:54.330
Salt dissolves only up to a limit, about

00:04:54.431 --> 00:04:57.016
thirty-six grams in a hundred millilitres of water.

00:04:57.979 --> 00:05:00.260
Write an inquiry question about the volume of

00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:00.941
water used,

00:05:01.462 --> 00:05:04.644
then a hypothesis using if, then, because.

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:06.282
Pause here to answer.

00:05:17.001 --> 00:05:19.627
One good question: how does the volume of

00:05:19.687 --> 00:05:22.293
water used affect the percentage of salt recovered

00:05:22.313 --> 00:05:22.935
from the mixture?

00:05:23.836 --> 00:05:24.538
A hypothesis:

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:27.243
if more water is used, then more salt

00:05:27.343 --> 00:05:29.988
is recovered, because more water can dissolve more

00:05:30.068 --> 00:05:32.332
salt, leaving less behind in the sand.

00:05:33.778 --> 00:05:36.696
More water is better, is the classic slip.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.615
It names nothing to measure, and gives no

00:05:39.697 --> 00:05:39.998
reason.

00:05:41.694 --> 00:05:42.142
Last one.

00:05:43.011 --> 00:05:46.845
The student tests twenty, forty, sixty and eighty

00:05:46.927 --> 00:05:48.010
millilitres of water.

00:05:48.693 --> 00:05:52.355
The salt recovered is thirty-five point five, sixty-nine

00:05:52.396 --> 00:05:55.718
point four, sixty-nine point six and sixty-nine point

00:05:55.839 --> 00:05:57.500
five percent of the mixture.

00:05:58.342 --> 00:06:01.369
Explain what this evidence shows, and modify the

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:02.873
hypothesis to reflect it.

00:06:03.597 --> 00:06:04.743
Pause here to answer.

00:06:15.415 --> 00:06:19.220
Recovery rises from twenty to forty millilitres, then

00:06:19.342 --> 00:06:21.124
stays at about seventy percent.

00:06:22.027 --> 00:06:25.418
Forty millilitres can dissolve about fourteen grams, so

00:06:25.499 --> 00:06:27.385
by then, all the salt has dissolved.

00:06:28.089 --> 00:06:28.980
So modify it.

00:06:29.628 --> 00:06:32.193
If more water is used, the salt recovered

00:06:32.354 --> 00:06:35.982
increases until all the salt dissolves, then stays

00:06:36.023 --> 00:06:38.187
the same, because extra water has no more

00:06:38.248 --> 00:06:39.009
salt to dissolve.

00:06:39.952 --> 00:06:41.736
Ignoring the last two results because they don't

00:06:41.857 --> 00:06:43.560
fit is the classic mistake.

00:06:44.284 --> 00:06:46.817
Evidence that doesn't fit is what improves the

00:06:46.858 --> 00:06:47.480
hypothesis.

00:06:49.448 --> 00:06:52.536
So an observation becomes an inquiry question that

00:06:52.558 --> 00:06:53.459
data can answer,

00:06:53.982 --> 00:06:56.348
then a hypothesis with a chemistry reason.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.334
And when evidence doesn't fit, we ask whether

00:06:59.375 --> 00:07:02.300
it challenges the method, the prediction or the

00:07:02.361 --> 00:07:02.681
reason.

00:07:03.645 --> 00:07:05.213
Our copper sulfate question is ready.

00:07:05.616 --> 00:07:05.836
Next,

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.232
how does a chemistry plan produce valid, useful

00:07:09.273 --> 00:07:09.594
data?

00:07:12.461 --> 00:07:13.284
Here are the key notes.

00:07:13.807 --> 00:07:15.355
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:15.395 --> 00:07:15.817
to copy them.
